# {{projectName}}

A standalone TinyGres browser database application built with TypeScript and
Vite.

## Start it

```sh
cd client
npm install
npm run dev
```

## What the app demonstrates

The app awaits TinyGres in its default dedicated module Worker, where the
page-native database engine runs in Rust/WASM. It uses the conventional
`create()`, `exec()`, `query()`, and `close()` API:

- one atomic `exec()` script creates the typed relational schema;
- parameterized `query()` calls perform local writes and reads;
- a callback transaction commits related writes atomically;
- a three-table join follows a many-to-many relationship;
- a single-table aggregate summarizes the stored data; and
- table subscriptions trigger a fresh query after a commit.

All database work is local. This starter has no network credentials, remote
service, or hidden network path.

{{#if usesOpfs}}
The database uses persistent OPFS storage with the logical name
`{{storageName}}`. Reloading the page reopens the same database without
re-seeding it. OPFS requires a secure browser context and permits one open
TinyGres Worker for a database name; close the client before another Worker
opens the same database.
{{else}}
The database uses memory storage and starts from a blank database on every page
load, so the demo bootstraps its schema and sample rows again. Choose OPFS when
the data should survive reloads.
{{/if}}

## Working with TinyGres

Use `query()` for one statement, including `$1` parameters for application
values. Use `exec()` for one or more parameter-free statements that should
succeed or fail as a single implicit transaction. The starter uses one such
script for its complete schema bootstrap. Use `transaction(callback)` to group
parameterized `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` statements; queries inside the
callback see its staged rows.

TinyGres deliberately implements a bounded PostgreSQL-shaped dialect rather
than a PostgreSQL server. The
[SQL compatibility contract](https://github.com/tinyplex/tinygres/blob/main/docs/sql.md)
lists the exact syntax, type semantics, unsupported features, and runtime
limits.

The client is closed during page teardown. Preserve that lifecycle when moving
the database into a larger application.
